The '''Chevrolet Corvette (C8)''' is the eighth generation of the [[Chevrolet Corvette|Corvette]] [[sports car]] manufactured by American automobile manufacturer [[Chevrolet]]. It is the first [[Rear mid-engine, rear-wheel-drive layout|rear mid-engine]] Corvette, departing from the longstanding [[Front-engine, rear-wheel-drive layout#Front mid-engine, rear-wheel-drive layout|front mid-engine]] design used for the previous seven generations since its introduction in 1953.

Two variants of the C8 would go racing globally. The base model Corvette would serve as the basis for the [[Chevrolet Corvette C8.R]], debuting in July 2019, later going on to win the [[2020 IMSA SportsCar Championship|2020]] and [[2021 IMSA SportsCar Championship]]s and the [[2023 FIA World Endurance Championship]].<ref name=":12">{{cite web |title=This Is the New Corvette C8.R Race Car |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/a28441596/2020-corvette-c8r-mid-engine-race-car/ |access-date=2019-09-19 |publisher=Road & Track}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rehman |first=Mohammed |date=July 13, 2023 |title=How Corvette Racing secured the final GTE title |url=https://www.motorsportweek.com/2023/07/13/how-corvette-racing-secured-the-final-gte-title/ |access-date=June 2, 2025 |website=Motorsportweek.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> It would later be succeeded by the Z06-based [[Chevrolet Corvette Z06 GT3.R]] in 2024, following the discontinuation of the [[LM GTE]] class.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The New Chevrolet Corvette Z06 GT3.R: More Road To Race Relevance Than Ever Before From Corvette |url=https://www.dailysportscar.com/2023/01/27/the-new-chevrolet-corvette-z06-gt3-r-more-road-to-race-relevance-than-ever-before-from-corvette.html |access-date=2023-08-22 |website=Dailysportscar.com |date=January 27, 2023 }}</ref>

== Development ==
GM and Chevrolet started developing the next-generation (C8) [[Mid-engine]] Corvette around 2016.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1103961_report-mid-engine-c8-corvette-to-bow-at-2018-detroit-audi-show|title=Report: Mid-engine C8 Corvette to bow at 2018 Detroit Auto Show|date=May 15, 2016|website=Motorauthority.com|access-date=August 3, 2026}}</ref> The car was initially planned for the 2018 model year before delaying it to 2019 and 2020.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.detroitnews.com/picture-gallery/business/autos/2018/06/05/the-next-corvette-pace-car-will-be-mid-engine/35713073/|title=The next Corvette pace car will be mid-engine|work=[[The Detroit News]]|date=June 5, 2018|access-date=July 20, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://drivemag.com/news/2019-chevrolet-corvette-c8-production-said-to-start-next-january/|title=2019 Chevrolet Corvette C8 production said to start next January |website=Drivemag.com|date=October 19, 2017|access-date=August 3, 2026}}</ref>

Chevrolet announced the C8 in April 2019 with teasers showing off the model in a camouflage form<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a27116013/chevrolet-corvette-c8-mid-engine-confirmed-2020/|title=The Mid-Engined 2020 Chevrolet Corvette C8 Is Real, GM Confesses, and It Will Debut July 18|date=April 29, 2019|website=Caranddriver.com|access-date=August 3, 2026}}</ref>

The 2020 coupe variant made its official debut on July 18, 2019 during a media event at Tustin, California (referencing its [[Chevrolet Corvette#NASA Corvettes|association with NASA]]) to coincide with the 50th anniversary of the [[Apollo 11]] mission, while the 2020 convertible variant made its debut in October 2019.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1124904_mid-engine-c8-corvette-convertible-to-be-revealed-in-october|title=Mid-engine C8 Corvette convertible to be revealed in October|date=September 5, 2019|website=Motorauthority.com|access-date=August 3, 2026}}</ref>

Production of the C8 was originally planned to begin in late 2019, but it officially began on February 3, 2020, delayed by the [[2019 General Motors strike]].<ref name="gma20200203"/><ref name="rt-jul2019"/>

== Overview ==
The Corvette C8 is GM's first rear mid-engine sports car since the [[Pontiac Fiero]]. It features a vastly different design from previous Corvettes, with an all-new aluminum architecture and [[coilover]] springs in place of the rear [[Corvette leaf spring|transverse mono-leaf spring]] used on previous models. The exterior features more aggressive aerodynamics including larger air intakes and prominent side scoops. A trunk is located at the rear, with additional storage space at the front of the car. Combined, these provide {{convert|13|cuft|L}} of cargo space, {{convert|2|cuft|L}} less than that of the C7.<ref name="dn-jul2019"/> As a result of the switch to a mid-engine layout, the passenger cell has been shifted forward by {{convert|16.5|in|mm}}.<ref name="et-jul2019"/> The cockpit has been designed to be driver-centric, with numerous controls mounted on the center console as well as utilizing a new hexagonal steering wheel. A {{cvt|12|in|cm|1}} digital screen replaces the instrument cluster and reflects one of the six selected driving modes, and is accompanied by an {{cvt|8|in|cm|1}} touchscreen. A special Z button is located on the steering wheel; named in honor of earlier performance packages like Z51, Z06, and ZR1, it allows the driver to quickly activate customized performance settings.<ref name="cd-jul2019"/>

