== Awards ==
The Corvette C8 was named [[Motor Trend Car of the Year|2020 ''Motor Trend'' Car of the Year]],<ref name="mt-nov2019"/> and was also featured on [[Car and Driver 10Best|2020 ''Car and Driver'' 10Best]].<ref name="cd-nov2019"/> The C8 was also named [[North American Car of the Year|2020 ''North American'' Car of the Year]],<ref name="cc-jan2020"/> [[Detroit Free Press|2020 ''Detroit Free Press'' Car of the Year]],<ref name="dfp-jan2020"/> [[MotorWeek Drivers' Choice Awards|2020 MotorWeek Drivers' Choice Best of the Year]],<ref name="mw-feb2020"/> and [[Edmunds (company)|Edmunds]] Top Rated Sports Car for 2020 <ref name="edmunds1"/> and 2021.<ref name="edmunds2"/>

The Corvette C8 Z06 won both the MotorTrend Performance Vehicle of the Year<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-01-20 |title=The Chevrolet Corvette Z06 Is the 2023 MotorTrend Performance Vehicle of the Year |url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/chevrolet-corvette-z06-2023-motortrend-performance-vehicle-of-the-year |access-date=2025-10-29 |website=MotorTrend |language=en}}</ref> and the Road & Track Performance Car of the Year,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Road & Track Staff |date=January 19, 2023 |title=2023 Performance Car of the Year |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/reviews/a42358903/2023-performance-car-of-the-year/ |access-date=Oct 29, 2025 |website=Road & Track Magazine}}</ref> both for 2023.

The Corvette C8 Stingray and E-Ray won the 2025 Car and Driver 10 Best.<ref name="cd-dec2024" />

== Motorsport ==

=== C8.R ===
{{Main|Chevrolet Corvette C8.R}}

[[File:2023 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps Corvette Racing Chevrolet Corvette C8.R No.33 (DSC00077).jpg|thumb|The [[Chevrolet Corvette C8.R]] at the [[2023 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps]].]]
The racing version of the base model C8, called the [[Chevrolet Corvette C8.R]], was unveiled in 2019 alongside the Corvette C8 convertible.<ref name=":12" /> Built to [[LM GTE]] regulations, the C8.R was the first mid-engined Corvette built for racing by [[Chevrolet]] and [[Pratt Miller Motorsports]], as well as the first Corvette race car to use a [[flat-plane crank]] [[V8 engine|V8]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=2020 Chevrolet Corvette C8.R race car unveiled |url=https://www.drive.com.au/news/2020-chevrolet-corvette-c8-r/ |access-date=2025-09-19 |website=Drive |language=en}}</ref> It completed its debut in the [[2020 24 Hours of Daytona]]. The C8.R won a total of three Teams' championships and three Drivers' championships during its tenure, before being retired in 2024 following the discontinuation of the LM GTE ruleset.

=== Z06 GT3.R ===
{{Main|Chevrolet Corvette Z06 GT3.R}}
[[File:2024 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps TF Sport Chevrolet Corvette Z06 GT3.R No.81 (DSC02653).jpg|thumb|The [[Chevrolet Corvette Z06 GT3.R]] at the [[2024 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps]].]]
The Z06-based [[Chevrolet Corvette Z06 GT3.R]] was first revealed in 2023, following a two-year testing program, beginning with virtual simulation tests in early 2021, later moving to on-track testing in 2022 and 2023.<ref name=":02">{{Cite web |title=Corvette Reveal New Z06 GT3.R {{!}} dailysportscar.com |url=https://www.dailysportscar.com/2023/01/27/corvette-reveal-new-z06-gt3-r.html |access-date=2023-08-22 |website=Dailysportscar.com |date=January 27, 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=WATCH: New Ford, Corvette GT3s On Track At Sebring (UPDATED) {{!}} dailysportscar.com |url=https://www.dailysportscar.com/2023/03/22/watch-new-ford-corvette-gt3s-on-track-at-sebring.html |access-date=2023-08-22 |website=Dailysportscar.com |date=March 22, 2023 }}</ref> It served as the successor to the C8.R and the [[Chevrolet Corvette (C7)#C7 GT3-R|Callaway Corvette C7 GT3-R]]. The Z06 GT3.R made its competitive debut at the [[2024 24 Hours of Daytona]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Corvette Z06 GT3.R made its worldwide competition debut to open the IMSA at Daytona |url=https://en.endurance-info.com/auto/article/110467-corvette-z06-gt3r-made-its-worldwide-competition-debut-open-imsa-daytona |access-date=2024-03-17 |website=Endurance Info |language=en}}</ref>
